\section{Step 1, fit the network meta-analysis}{


\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{library(netmeta); library(nmaplots)
data(mash)
d <- mash$fib_improvement_alldoses
p <- pairwise(treat = treatment, event = responders, n = sampleSize,
              studlab = study, data = d, sm = "RR")
net <- netmeta(p, reference.group = "Placebo")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{mash} ships with the package: arm-level data, one row per study arm,
which is what \code{pairwise()} takes. Any \code{netmeta} object works. Sample sizes
appear on the plot when the object carries them (\code{n1}/\code{n2}, which
\code{pairwise()} supplies when you give \code{n}); otherwise the number of studies
per treatment is shown as \code{k = ...}.

Fitting with \pkg{gemtc} instead? Pass the \code{mtc.network}, the
\code{mtc.model} or the \code{mtc.run()} result straight to \code{\link[=nmaplot]{nmaplot()}}:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{ids <- gsub("[^A-Za-z0-9_]", "_", d$treatment)
network <- gemtc::mtc.network(
  data.ab = data.frame(study = d$study, treatment = ids,
                       responders = d$responders, sampleSize = d$sampleSize),
  treatments = unique(data.frame(id = ids, description = d$treatment)))
nmaplot(network, reference = "Placebo")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

gemtc ids allow only letters, digits and underscore; the \code{description}
column carries the readable names and becomes the node labels.
}

\section{Step 2, draw}{


\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{nmaplot(net, outcome = "Fibrosis improvement without worsening of MASH")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Defaults: treatments on a polygon (\code{layout = "multi"}), node area
proportional to the sample size, reference treatment in grey and the
others in muted red, black edges whose width follows the number of direct
studies with that number in a white circle on the edge, bold treatment name
with \verb{event/n = events/participants (event rate \%)} underneath (\code{n = ...}
without events, \code{k = ...} without sample sizes), each node's share of all
participants printed in bold inside the disc, centred title
"Network of Interventions" with
the outcome as subtitle, and a boxed legend under the network.

The result is a \code{ggplot}; \code{+} works as usual to add layers or change the
theme. The tables used for drawing are in \code{p$nmaplot$nodes},
\code{p$nmaplot$edges} and \code{p$nmaplot$rings}.
}

\section{Step 3, save}{


\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{nmaplot(net, outcome = "Fibrosis improvement without worsening of MASH",
        file = c("network.png", "network.pdf", "network.tiff"),
        width = 9, height = 9, dpi = 300)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The format follows the extension. PDF is vector (Cairo), PNG and TIFF use
\pkg{ragg} when installed. \code{width} and \code{height} are inches; a square or
slightly tall figure suits the legend below the network.
}

